892 0 obj
<<
/Title (A retail bank modernizing its operations chose a hybrid strategy: the core banking engine and the customer-facing mobile banking app, which required deep customization, high performance, and strict security, were built using traditional coding, while internal tools such as loan application intake, document verification requests, and branch service forms were built using a low-code platform. This allowed the core engineering team to focus their traditional coding effort on the systems that provided the greatest competitive differentiation, while business teams were empowered to build and maintain their own lightweight internal tools.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 856 0 R
>>
endobj
893 0 obj
<<
/Title <>
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 857 0 R
/Next 894 0 R
>>
endobj
894 0 obj
<<
/Title (Traditional coding, by contrast, relies on established software engineering roles such as backend developers, frontend developers, DevOps engineers, and quality assurance specialists, each contributing specialized expertise. This structure supports rigorous code review, automated testing, and formal release management processes, which are often critical for regulated industries and mission-critical systems.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 857 0 R
/Prev 893 0 R
>>
endobj
895 0 obj
<<
/Title (18.1 Risks Associated with Low-Code)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 858 0 R
/First 916 0 R
